// pattern under a DOUBLE reference — `match & &p { P { x, y } => … }` — is legal Rust
// (the pattern peels every reference layer and the bindings are `&i64`) and TODAY it
// compiles clean, rc 0, and READS A STACK ADDRESS: measured 2026-09-05 with a print,
// `x=140721330920408 y=140721330920408` where Rust gives 3 and 4. The one-layer twin
// (`match &p`) in the same program is CORRECT, which is the whole shape of the defect:
// the PAT_STRUCT door of build_pattern_impl (sema_stmt.cpp, `if (pc == la::PAT_STRUCT)`)
// never peels the scrutinee at all — it only compares the struct NAME, and a Ref
// scrutinee skips even that check — so the second layer is never dereferenced and the
